multichat.db

# ANCHORED. This rule exists for the stale ELF binary at the repo root, but written
# bare as "server" it also matched cmd/server/ -- THE MAIN PACKAGE'S OWN SOURCE
# DIRECTORY -- because an unanchored pattern matches at every level. cmd/server/main.go
# survives only because it was tracked before the rule existed; the next file added to
# the main package would have been silently ignored, never shipped, and the committed
# tree would no longer build while `git status` looked perfectly clean. A "!*.go"
# exception cannot rescue this: git will not re-include a file inside an excluded
# directory. Anchoring is the only fix.
/server

# The compiled server. It was COMMITTED as a 13 MB ELF binary alongside its own
# source until 2026-07-13, so every build left the tree dirty and invited someone to
# commit the new one. Anchored with a leading slash on purpose: a bare
# "multichat-server" would also match a directory of that name anywhere in the tree.
/multichat-server



# The sabotage engine creates this on import
scripts/__pycache__/
